---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

// Project page for the DFKI mechanistic-interpretability collaboration (B4). A
// bounded pilot and training in explaining model decisions through behavioural
// controls and internal interventions. Xiaochuan's role is experimental design. Red lines: the
// project is never called "LiT" on the site; no collaborator is named (DFKI as an
// institution is already public on the CV/homepage); the method is stated modestly
// and no results are claimed — the minimal evidence chain is still being assembled.
---

<Base
  title="Latent Control States · Xiaochuan Li"
  description="Training in mechanistic interpretability through a collaboration with DFKI: controlled behavioural comparisons and proposed internal interventions to explain how prompt framing affects model decisions."
  showProgress={true}
  toc={true}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="!mb-6 !text-sm"><a href="/research" class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">← Current Work</a></p>
    <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-dim)] font-medium mb-2">
      Collaboration · DFKI · behavioural pilots
    </div>
    <h1>Prompt framing and latent control states in language models</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] italic !text-lg !mb-3">
      Which internal representations and computations contribute to a model’s choice when prompt
      framing changes its answer?
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-dim)] !mb-8">
      {projects.latentControl.summary}
    </p>

    <aside class="rounded-r-lg px-4 py-3 !my-8 [border-top:1px_solid_var(--color-border)] [border-right:1px_solid_var(--color-border)] [border-bottom:1px_solid_var(--color-border)] [border-left:3px_solid_var(--color-accent)] [background:var(--color-surface)]">
      <p class="!mt-0 !mb-1.5 text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.13em] text-[var(--color-text-dim)] font-medium" style="font-family:var(--font-mono)">Place in the research trajectory</p>
      <p class="!my-0 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">{projects.latentControl.connection}</p>
    </aside>

    <p>
      The behavioural pilots examine how moral framing affects choices in matched dilemmas.
      Several explanations remain possible: the model may misread the outcomes, retrieve a familiar
      response, or favour an action despite correctly representing its consequences. The mechanism
      question is which information and computations actually contribute to the choice.
    </p>

    <h2>The design</h2>
    <p>
      The setup is a utilitarian minimal-pair experiment. Each pair holds the moral scenario fixed and
      varies one thing at a time — the dilemma, the size of the benefit at stake, and the framing of the
      prompt — so a difference in the model’s decision can be traced to a specific manipulation rather
      than to unrelated content. Keeping the pair matched is what lets an internal difference be read as
      caused by framing rather than by a change of topic.
    </p>

    <h2>Methods training</h2>
    <p>
      The project trains a sequence of research skills: establishing a behavioural contrast,
      separating competing explanations, identifying candidate internal representations, and
      designing interventions that test their role in the answer. The current materials distinguish
      outcome sensitivity from wording, framing, and action preferences. The next tests check whether
      the model understands the stated consequences before interpreting an internal intervention.
    </p>
    <p>
      A representation may contain decodable information without the model using that information
      for the task. The proposed interventions therefore ask whether changing a candidate state
      selectively changes the behavioural contrast while preserving comprehension, with control
      directions and new materials used to assess alternative explanations.
    </p>

    <h2>The test</h2>
    <p>
      The evidence chain is deliberately minimal and direct. Rather than a full circuit analysis, it
      targets one question: can the direction in the model’s activations that tracks framing be added or
      removed, and does doing so change the decision? A necessity-and-sufficiency check of that kind is
      a candidate test, provided matched control directions and independent materials rule out a
      nonspecific change in output preference. A steering effect alone does not identify the full
      decision mechanism. More detailed analysis would depend on the result of that controlled test.
    </p>

    <h2>Connection to language research</h2>
    <p>
      I aim to carry this method into studies of linguistic meaning and inference. Once a semantic
      distinction produces a reliable model behaviour, internal analysis can ask which information
      supports it and whether a proposed computation survives targeted tests. This complements
      formal analysis, human experiments, and explicit model building. Any connection to human
      processing would require independent human evidence.
    </p>

    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p class="cw-status">
      Behavioural pilots and matched materials are in place. They motivated controls separating
      outcome sensitivity from action preferences and framing. Mechanistic tests are being developed;
      a causal explanation of the behavioural effect has not yet been established.
    </p>

    <p class="!mt-10 !text-sm !text-[var(--color-text-dim)]">
      <a href="/research" class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">← Back to Current Work</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

